            <ArticleTitle>Symptomatic pelvic accessory spleen in a female adolescent: Case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>600-602</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1002/jcu.22448</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Accessory spleens are congenital foci of healthy splenic tissue that are separate from the main body of the spleen. They occur in 10-30% of individuals; a wandering accessory spleen located in the pelvis is uncommon. Most patients are asymptomatic. We describe the case of a female adolescent presenting with acute abdominal pain who had a pelvic accessory spleen that mimicked an adnexal mass. © 2017 Wiley Periodicals, Inc. J Clin Ultrasound 45:600-602, 2017.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
